 be the image of the rectangle shown above under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef668553-5d10-5944-a4d2-e24693f30a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1080 "\\ne " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03ae9695-f75b-5e80-9b1c-90cce0d389b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1080 "\\le " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04e9760d-e45c-5051-bbd0-2a36daa0f878" #edb/ref 17592